$ enola check --help
exit: 2
--- stdout
--- stderr
Usage: enola check [flags] [config_path]

Grade what a change did to the architecture, against a pinned baseline.

Nothing fails until you say what should: with no --fail-on and no --max-spillover
every finding is reported and the run exits 0. The names --fail-on accepts are
exactly these, and are case-sensitive:
  cycles, layers, crossrepo, coverage, unused-routes, messaging-coverage, god-class, hotspots, dependency-depth, exported-surface, complexity-outliers, intent, constraints, domain, query-loops, entry-points, dead-methods, vendored-candidates, import-closure, package-metrics, dead-code, performance

Exit codes:
  0  clean      nothing the policy enforces
  1  regression the policy was violated
  2  error      the gate could not run (no baseline, bad flag, inverted pair)
  3  declined   the baseline is not comparable; refusing to grade

Flags:
  -author string
    	with --reviewers, whose change this is (default: git config user.name)
  -baseline string
    	what to compare against: pinned, previous, or a path to a directory holding facts.jsonl (default "pinned")
  -detail
    	print the full delta (changed edges and facts) under the verdict
  -expected string
    	comma-separated packages you expected to touch, in addition to the predicted radius
  -fail-on string
    	comma-separated explainer names whose new findings fail (default: none — the run reports and exits 0)
  -focus string
    	narrow the delta to entries referencing this module/file/symbol
  -format string
    	how to write the verdict: text, json, sarif, or annotations (default "text")
  -host string
    	with -format annotations, the CI that shows them: buildkite or github
  -json
    	emit the verdict as JSON instead of text (an alias of -format json)
  -link string
    	with -host buildkite, the pull request's files view to link each line into
  -max-spillover int
    	fail when more than N packages are reached outside the declared scope (default: report only, never fail) (default -1)
  -min-confidence float
    	confidence floor within --fail-on explainers (default: 1.00)
  -reviewer-window int
    	with --reviewers, how many recent commits authorship is measured over (default 500)
  -reviewers
    	report who owns the modules this change touched, and who should review it (reads git author names)
  -target string
    	the symbol, type or package you INTENDED to change; packages reached outside its predicted blast radius are reported as spillover
  -warn-only
    	downgrade a --fail-on / --max-spillover policy to warnings (blocking/usage errors still apply)
  -write
    	persist snapshot artifacts to .enola/ (default: read-only, nothing is written)
